  "scriptId": "am-08-multi-ingredient-advanced",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Multi-Ingredient and Advanced Mixture Puzzles",
    "description": "Choose the right model for three-component, profit-linked, and multi-step mixture problems.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach method selection before calculation. Use weighted average for value problems, direct component balance for add/remove stories, and alligation after collapsing subgroups when possible. Progress through three-component ratios, combined varieties, unknown ingredient quantities, and a capstone that joins component bookkeeping to a target mean.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach method selection for the chapter's harder mixture questions. Cover three ingredients in a fixed ratio, removing a proportional sample and then adding selected pure ingredients, finding an unknown third variety price when two known varieties are first combined, and cost/profit problems where one ingredient quantity is known and the other must be found from the target mixture cost. Include percentage-profit alligation as a weighted-average application and multi-component composition equations in the style of grape-pineapple-banana questions. Learners should first ask: is this a weighted average, a one-component add/remove problem, or a component-balance equation? The capstone should combine ratio bookkeeping with a mean-value condition."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's tackle the mixture questions that look crowded.\n\nThree juices, two prices plus profit, or a remove-and-add story can seem like different topics. The real skill is deciding what is being conserved: total value, one component, or a weighted mean.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Three-component ratios are easiest when turned into actual parts.\n\nGrape:pineapple:banana = 4:6:5.\nTotal parts = 15.\n\nSo every 15 L sample contains 4 L grape, 6 L pineapple, 5 L banana.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A well-mixed sample keeps the same 4:6:5 composition.\n\nIf 30 L is removed, twice as much leaves: 8 L, 12 L, and 10 L.\n\nThis proportional-removal idea is the base for harder three-juice questions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
