    "remedy_2": {
      "id": "remedy_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common slip here is to mix up which number is a price, quantity, or fraction.\n\nLet amounts be 2x,3x,5x. A 10 L sample removes 2,3,5.\n\nFinal A=2x−2+4=2x+2; B=3x−3+1=3x−2. Difference=x−4=5, so x=9 and total=10x=90 L.\n\nNow use the same idea on fresh values.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "retry_remedy_2": {
      "id": "retry_remedy_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's rebuild this one from the quantities in the question.\n\nFinal A=3x−3+5=3x+2; B=2x−2. Difference=x+4=7, so x=3 and total=10x=30 L.\n\nTry it again with that relationship in view.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Three price varieties can sometimes be collapsed.\n\nSuppose varieties at ₹120 and ₹140 are used in equal amounts. Their subgroup mean is ₹130.\n\nIf the third variety is used in twice that subgroup amount, the whole problem becomes ₹130 versus ₹x in ratio 1:2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3_step": {
      "id": "teach_3_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For final mean ₹160:\n(1×130 + 2x)/3 = 160\n130 + 2x = 480\n2x=350 → x=₹175.\n\nCollapsing a balanced subgroup reduces clutter without changing the weighted average.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_3"
        }
      ]
    },
